{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ac49164eaf8bc96e", "type": "tool_result", "content": "0\tMMAG: A Multi-Control Mixed Audio Generation Benchmark\n1\tTitle:\n2\tContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n3\tDescription:\n4\tarXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n5\tLearn more\n6\t×\n7\tLicense: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n8\tarXiv:2608.06900v1 [cs.SD] 07 Aug 2026\n9\tMMAG: A Multi-Control Mixed Audio Generation Benchmark\n10\tZihao Zheng\n11\t1,2\n12\tXuenan Xu\n13\t2\n14\tJiahao Mei\n15\t1\n16\tYixuan Li\n17\t1,2\n18\tMinghao Lv\n19\t1\n20\tWen Wu\n21\t2\n22\tChao Zhang\n23\t2\n24\tMengyue Wu\n25\t1\n26\t1\n27\tMoE Key Lab of Artificial Intelligence, X-LANCE Lab, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, China\n28\t2\n29\tShanghai AI Lab, China\n30\trookie9@sjtu.edu.cn, mengyuewu@sjtu.edu.cn\n31\tCorresponding author.\n32\tAbstract\n33\tRecent audio generation systems have progressed from single-modality synthesis to generating complex acoustic scenes containing speech, music, and sound effects.\n34\tTherefore, evaluating these models requires assessing multiple interacting capabilities, including semantic fidelity, speaker consistency, and temporal control, yet existing benchmarks focus on isolated domains or coarse-grained descriptions.\n35\tTo address this gap, we introduce the Multi-control Mixed Audio Generation (MMAG) benchmark.\n36\tMMAG contains approximately 4,000 manually verified audio clips with rich annotations covering speech content, speaker identity, music attributes, sound events, and temporal relationships, together with dedicated subsets for voice cloning and timestamp-conditioned generation.\n37\tWe further propose a systematic evaluation protocol that measures acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic alignment, and temporal accuracy.\n38\tBenchmarking representative agentic orchestrators, unified audio-visual generation models, and native mixed-audio generators reveals substantial performance trade-offs across these capabilities, with no existing model performing consistently well.\n39\tOur results highlight the remaining challenges of controllable mixed audio generation and establish MMAG as a comprehensive benchmark for future research.\n40\t1\n41\t1\n42\t1\n43\tThe project page is available:\n44\thttps://hirookie9.github.io/MMAG-Page/\n45\tMMAG: A Multi-Control Mixed Audio Generation Benchmark\n46\tZihao Zheng\n47\t1,2\n48\tXuenan Xu\n49\t2\n50\tJiahao Mei\n51\t1\n52\tYixuan Li\n53\t1,2\n54\tMinghao Lv\n55\t1\n56\tWen Wu\n57\t2\n58\tChao Zhang\n59\t2\n60\tMengyue Wu\n61\t1\n62\t†\n63\t†\n64\tthanks:\n65\tCorresponding author.\n66\t1\n67\tMoE Key Lab of Artificial Intelligence, X-LANCE Lab, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, China\n68\t2\n69\tShanghai AI Lab, China\n70\trookie9@sjtu.edu.cn, mengyuewu@sjtu.edu.cn\n71\t1\n72\tIntroduction\n73\tResearch in audio generation has historically progressed along three parallel tracks, targeting speech, music, and sound effects separately.\n74\tEach track has developed its own specialized model architectures and datasets, with little cross-domain interaction\n75\t(Hung\n76\tet al.\n77\t,\n78\t2026\n79\t; Zhou\n80\tet al.\n81\t,\n82\t2026\n83\t; Copet\n84\tet al.\n85\t,\n86\t2023\n87\t)\n88\t.\n89\tWith the development of generative models, several works have started to integrate different audio domains. These efforts fall into three categories: agentic orchestrators that compose scenes via expert models, unified audio-visual generators that produce synchronized video-audio, and native mixed-audio generators that unify speech, music, and effects in a single end-to-end system.\n90\tTable 1:\n91\tComparison of MMAG with existing benchmarks. Domain columns indicate the audio categories covered. Condition columns indicate the types of annotations or control signals provided.\n92\t✓\n93\tindicates availability.\n94\t✗\n95\tindicates unavailability.\n96\tBenchmark\n97\tSize\n98\tDomain\n99\tCondition\n100\tSpeech\n101\tSound\n102\tMusic\n103\tTranscript\n104\tSpeaker Attr.\n105\tSound Event\n106\tMusic Info\n107\tTemporal Order\n108\tVoice Prompt\n109\tTimestamp\n110\tLibriSpeech-PC\n111\t1.2k\n112\t✓\n113\t✗\n114\t✗\n115\t✓\n116\t✗\n117\t✗\n118\t✗\n119\t✗\n120\t✓\n121\t✗\n122\tMusicBench\n123\t0.4k\n124\t✗\n125\t✗\n126\t✓\n127\t✗\n128\t✗\n129\t✗\n130\t✓\n131\t✗\n132\t✗\n133\t✗\n134\tAudioCaps\n135\t4.8k\n136\t✓\n137\t✓\n138\t✗\n139\t✗\n140\t✗\n141\t✓\n142\t✗\n143\t✓\n144\t✗\n145\t✗\n146\tMECAT\n147\t20k\n148\t✓\n149\t✓\n150\t✓\n151\t✓\n152\t✓\n153\t✓\n154\t✓\n155\t✗\n156\t✗\n157\t✗\n158\t\\rowcolor\n159\tgray!10\n160\tMMAG (Ours)\n161\t4k\n162\t✓\n163\t✓\n164\t✓\n165\t✓\n166\t✓\n167\t✓\n168\t✓\n169\t✓\n170\t0.7k\n171\t✓\n172\t1.8k\n173\t✓\n174\tThe emergence of mixed audio generation models poses new challenges for existing benchmarks.\n175\tThe first challenge lies in limited audio content and textual annotations.\n176\tMost existing benchmarks are designed for single-domain tasks: Text-to-Speech (TTS) test sets such as LibriSpeech-PC\n177\t(Meister\n178\tet al.\n179\t,\n180\t2023\n181\t)\n182\tcontain only clean speech, and Text-to-Music (TTM) benchmarks like Music-Bench\n183\t(Melechovsky\n184\tet al.\n185\t,\n186\t2024\n187\t)\n188\tconsist solely of instrumental music, neither covering cross-domain content.\n189\tIn contrast, Text-to-Audio (TTA) test sets such as AudioCaps\n190\t(Kim\n191\tet al.\n192\t,\n193\t2019\n194\t)\n195\tdo include speech and sound elements but suffer from coarse captions that omit speech transcriptions and speaker details.\n196\tMECAT\n197\t(Niu\n198\tet al.\n199\t,\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f533bf06-cac2-4284-a5b9-fefe61b594df", "uuid": "09961e54-51e3-4267-b420-19c1206cdec5", "timestamp": "2026-08-10T06:07:50.126Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mmag_text.txt", "content": "MMAG: A Multi-Control Mixed Audio Generation Benchmark\nTitle:\nContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nDescription:\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\nLearn more\n×\nLicense: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\narXiv:2608.06900v1 [cs.SD] 07 Aug 2026\nMMAG: A Multi-Control Mixed Audio Generation Benchmark\nZihao Zheng\n1,2\nXuenan Xu\n2\nJiahao Mei\n1\nYixuan Li\n1,2\nMinghao Lv\n1\nWen Wu\n2\nChao Zhang\n2\nMengyue Wu\n1\n1\nMoE Key Lab of Artificial Intelligence, X-LANCE Lab, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, China\n2\nShanghai AI Lab, China\nrookie9@sjtu.edu.cn, mengyuewu@sjtu.edu.cn\nCorresponding author.\nAbstract\nRecent audio generation systems have progressed from single-modality synthesis to generating complex acoustic scenes containing speech, music, and sound effects.\nTherefore, evaluating these models requires assessing multiple interacting capabilities, including semantic fidelity, speaker consistency, and temporal control, yet existing benchmarks focus on isolated domains or coarse-grained descriptions.\nTo address this gap, we introduce the Multi-control Mixed Audio Generation (MMAG) benchmark.\nMMAG contains approximately 4,000 manually verified audio clips with rich annotations covering speech content, speaker identity, music attributes, sound events, and temporal relationships, together with dedicated subsets for voice cloning and timestamp-conditioned generation.\nWe further propose a systematic evaluation protocol that measures acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic alignment, and temporal accuracy.\nBenchmarking representative agentic orchestrators, unified audio-visual generation models, and native mixed-audio generators reveals substantial performance trade-offs across these capabilities, with no existing model performing consistently well.\nOur results highlight the remaining challenges of controllable mixed audio generation and establish MMAG as a comprehensive benchmark for future research.\n1\n1\n1\nThe project page is available:\nhttps://hirookie9.github.io/MMAG-Page/\nMMAG: A Multi-Control Mixed Audio Generation Benchmark\nZihao Zheng\n1,2\nXuenan Xu\n2\nJiahao Mei\n1\nYixuan Li\n1,2\nMinghao Lv\n1\nWen Wu\n2\nChao Zhang\n2\nMengyue Wu\n1\n†\n†\nthanks:\nCorresponding author.\n1\nMoE Key Lab of Artificial Intelligence, X-LANCE Lab, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, China\n2\nShanghai AI Lab, China\nrookie9@sjtu.edu.cn, mengyuewu@sjtu.edu.cn\n1\nIntroduction\nResearch in audio generation has historically progressed along three parallel tracks, targeting speech, music, and sound effects separately.\nEach track has developed its own specialized model architectures and datasets, with little cross-domain interaction\n(Hung\net al.\n,\n2026\n; Zhou\net al.\n,\n2026\n; Copet\net al.\n,\n2023\n)\n.\nWith the development of generative models, several works have started to integrate different audio domains. These efforts fall into three categories: agentic orchestrators that compose scenes via expert models, unified audio-visual generators that produce synchronized video-audio, and native mixed-audio generators that unify speech, music, and effects in a single end-to-end system.\nTable 1:\nComparison of MMAG with existing benchmarks. Domain columns indicate the audio categories covered. Condition columns indicate the types of annotations or control signals provided.\n✓\nindicates availability.\n✗\nindicates unavailability.\nBenchmark\nSize\nDomain\nCondition\nSpeech\nSound\nMusic\nTranscript\nSpeaker Attr.\nSound Event\nMusic Info\nTemporal Order\nVoice Prompt\nTimestamp\nLibriSpeech-PC\n1.2k\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✗\n✗\n✓\n✗\nMusicBench\n0.4k\n✗\n✗\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✗\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✗\nAudioCaps\n4.8k\n✓\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✗\n✓\n✗\n✓\n✗\n✗\nMECAT\n20k\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✗\n\\rowcolor\ngray!10\nMMAG (Ours)\n4k\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n✓\n0.7k\n✓\n1.8k\n✓\nThe emergence of mixed audio generation models poses new challenges for existing benchmarks.\nThe first challenge lies in limited audio content and textual annotations.\nMost existing benchmarks are designed for single-domain tasks: Text-to-Speech (TTS) test sets such as LibriSpeech-PC\n(Meister\net al.\n,\n2023\n)\ncontain only clean speech, and Text-to-Music (TTM) benchmarks like Music-Bench\n(Melechovsky\net al.\n,\n2024\n)\nconsist solely of instrumental music, neither covering cross-domain content.\nIn contrast, Text-to-Audio (TTA) test sets such as AudioCaps\n(Kim\net al.\n,\n2019\n)\ndo include speech and sound elements but suffer from coarse captions that omit speech transcriptions and speaker details.\nMECAT\n(Niu\net al.\n,", "numLines": 200, "startLine": 0, "totalLines": 2810}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1786342071485", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c8ad33fd1d109cf", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/tmp/mmag_text.txt", "limit": 300, "offset": 200}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f533bf06-cac2-4284-a5b9-fefe61b594df", "uuid": "84691e5f-9b47-412e-b7b6-62f8dabef10d"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c8ad33fd1d109cf", "type": "tool_result", "content": "200\t,\n201\t2026\n202\t)\n203\tproduces richer captions for mixed scenes, yet it lacks temporal ordering of sound events for generation, which would require a more comprehensive annotation pipeline.\n204\tThe second challenge is the absence of fine-grained control mechanisms in existing benchmarks, as users in practice demand not only high-quality audio but also precise controllability over specific attributes.\n205\tOne example is zero-shot voice cloning in TTS\n206\t(Zhou\n207\tet al.\n208\t,\n209\t2026\n210\t)\n211\t, where the model mimics a speaker’s timbre from a short voice prompt.\n212\tThis necessitates that the benchmark provide suitable prompt samples.\n213\tHowever, selecting such segments from real-world audio is challenging due to complex acoustic scenes.\n214\tAnother example is temporal control in TTA, which requires the model to generate sound events at precisely specified timestamps\n215\t(Xie\n216\tet al.\n217\t,\n218\t2025\n219\t)\n220\t.\n221\tThis requires obtaining precise timestamps for each element during the annotation process.\n222\tTo address these challenges, we introduce MMAG, a benchmark for Multi-control Mixed Audio Generation.\n223\tWe construct three evaluation settings that differ in conditioning signals: (1) text-only control on the main set, (2) extra speaker identity control via a short voice prompt, and (3) temporal control via timestamp-detailed captions.\n224\tWe select cross-domain audio samples from test sets of AudioCaps, VGGSound, and MECAT.\n225\tExtending the multi-expert annotation pipeline of MECAT with a timestamp-labeling branch, we construct detailed captions including speech transcriptions, speaker attributes (age, gender, dialect, and emotion), sound event descriptions, musical information (genre, instrument and mood) and temporal ordering.\n226\tAfter manual inspection, the main set, voice cloning subset, and timestamp subset comprise around 3,980, 690, and 1,800 audio-text pairs respectively, with the latter two providing reference voices and precise temporal boundaries accordingly.\n227\tTable\n228\t1\n229\tcompares MMAG with existing benchmarks across coverage of domains and conditions.\n230\tUsing MMAG, we systematically evaluate representative models from these emerging system families.\n231\tTo provide a holistic assessment of mixed audio generation, we establish an evaluation protocol across different dimensions: acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic consistency, and temporal controllability.\n232\tOur findings reveal that existing models struggle to achieve balanced performance across different metrics.\n233\tMoreover, introducing voice prompts leads to a noticeable performance drop, and most models exhibit little capability for fine-grained temporal control.\n234\tOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n235\t1.\n236\tWe introduce\n237\tMMAG\n238\t, the first benchmark for\n239\tcompositional evaluation\n240\tof mixed audio generation. MMAG comprises a manually verified benchmark with rich annotations spanning speech, music, sound effects, and temporal relationships, together with dedicated subsets for prompt-based voice cloning and timestamp-conditioned generation.\n241\t2.\n242\tWe establish a unified evaluation framework that jointly measures acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic consistency and temporal control, providing a holistic assessment of mixed audio generation beyond conventional single-metric evaluation.\n243\t3.\n244\tWe benchmark representative agentic orchestrators, unified audio-visual models, and native mixed-audio generators under this unified protocol, revealing that current systems exhibit substantial trade-offs across generation quality and controllability, with no single model consistently excelling across all evaluation dimensions.\n245\t2\n246\tRelated Work\n247\tFigure 1:\n248\tOverview of the MMAG annotation pipeline. Expert models extract fine-grained attributes from audio clips. An LLM aggregates these annotations into coherent overall and timestamped captions, while speech segments are further processed to construct voice prompts for voice cloning evaluation. Human inspection is performed to ensure annotation quality.\n249\tBenchmarks for audio generation\n250\tExisting benchmarks for audio generation include AudioCaps\n251\t(Kim\n252\tet al.\n253\t,\n254\t2019\n255\t)\n256\tand VGGSound\n257\t(Chen\n258\tet al.\n259\t,\n260\t2020\n261\t)\n262\t, which provide large-scale real-world audio with coarse annotations.\n263\tand MECAT\n264\t(Niu\n265\tet al.\n266\t,\n267\t2026\n268\t)\n269\t, built from ACAV100M\n270\t(Lee\n271\tet al.\n272\t,\n273\t2021\n274\t)\n275\t, which also serves as a valuable audio source and offers richer annotations through expert-model and LLM combination.\n276\tHowever, none of these resources are designed for compositional evaluation of mixed audio generation.\n277\tEvaluation of audio generation\n278\tExisting metrics for audio generation have been developed along separate lines, each targeting a specific quality dimension. For distributional metrics, Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD)\n279\t(Kilgour\n280\tet al.\n281\t,\n282\t2018\n283\t)\n284\t, Fréchet Distance (FD), Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, and Inception Score (IS)\n285\t(Salimans\n286\tet al.\n287\t,\n288\t2016\n289\t)\n290\tare widely used to quantify how closely the generated audio matches the real distribution. Alongside these metrics, no-reference predictors such as Audiobox Aesthetics\n291\t(Tjandra\n292\tet al.\n293\t,\n294\t2025\n295\t)\n296\tprovide perceptual quality assessments across multiple axes. For speech metrics, Word Error Rate (WER), speaker similarity (SPK-SIM), and UTMOS\n297\t2\n298\t2\n299\t2\n300\tUTMOS-v2\n301\t(Baba\n302\tet al.\n303\t,\n304\t2024\n305\t)\n306\t.\n307\taddress transcription accuracy, identity preservation, and naturalness respectively. For semantic metrics, Contrastive Language-Audio Pretraining (CLAP) score\n308\t(Wu\n309\tet al.\n310\t,\n311\t2023\n312\t)\n313\tprovides global text-audio alignment, while AnyAudio-Judge\n314\t(Li\n315\tet al.\n316\t,\n317\t2026\n318\t)\n319\tachieves fine-grained semantic evaluation by decomposing captions into atomic rubrics.\n320\tWhile these metrics cover complementary aspects of quality, they remain scattered across isolated benchmarks, with no unified protocol for evaluating controllable mixed audio generation.\n321\tAudio generation systems\n322\tRecent audio generation systems can be grouped into three paradigms.\n323\tAgentic orchestrators\n324\t, represented in our evaluation by AuDirector\n325\t(Ren\n326\tet al.\n327\t,\n328\t2026\n329\t)\n330\t, employ LLMs as central agents to invoke expert models including TangoFlux\n331\t(Hung\n332\tet al.\n333\t,\n334\t2026\n335\t)\n336\t, IndexTTS2\n337\t(Zhou\n338\tet al.\n339\t,\n340\t2026\n341\t)\n342\t, and MusicGen\n343\t(Copet\n344\tet al.\n345\t,\n346\t2023\n347\t)\n348\t.\n349\tAuDirector further incorporates critic models such as CLAP and MiMo-Audio\n350\t(Xiaomi,\n351\t2025\n352\t)\n353\tto iteratively evaluate and refine generated audio quality.\n354\tThis training-free design enables flexible adaptation to diverse conditioning inputs, including voice references or timestamps.\n355\tUnified audio-visual generation models\n356\taccept diverse input modalities and synthesize synchronized audio-visual content.\n357\tRepresentative models in our evaluation include MOVA\n358\t(SII-OpenMOSS Team\n359\tet al.\n360\t,\n361\t2026\n362\t)\n363\t, Ovi\n364\t(Low\n365\tet al.\n366\t,\n367\t2025\n368\t)\n369\t, UniAVGen\n370\t(Zhang\n371\tet al.\n372\t,\n373\t2025\n374\t)\n375\t, LTX-2\n376\t(HaCohen\n377\tet al.\n378\t,\n379\t2026\n380\t)\n381\t, and JavisDiT++\n382\t(Liu\n383\tet al.\n384\t,\n385\t2026\n386\t)\n387\t, which can synthesize synchronized video alongside general audio covering speech, music, and sound effects.\n388\tNative mixed-audio generators\n389\t, represented by Dasheng AudioGen\n390\t(Mei\n391\tet al.\n392\t,\n393\t2026\n394\t)\n395\t, synthesize mixed audio scenes from text.\n396\tMing-Omni-TTS\n397\t(Inclusion AI\n398\tet al.\n399\t,\n400\t2025\n401\t)\n402\t, a speech-centric system derived from TTS, extends its scope to joint audio-music generation.\n403\tThese increasingly capable systems motivate a benchmark capable of evaluating multiple control dimensions simultaneously.\n404\t3\n405\tConstructing MMAG\n406\tMMAG is designed to enable\n407\tcompositional evaluation\n408\tof mixed audio generation, where speech, music, and sound effects must be generated coherently within a single acoustic scene. Rather than introducing another collection of audio-text pairs, our construction process is guided by three principles: (1) preserving realistic mixed-audio scenarios, (2) providing rich semantic and temporal annotations for fine-grained control, and (3) enabling multi-condition control through dedicated subsets.\n409\tFigure\n410\t1\n411\tillustrates the overall construction process.\n412\tFigure 2:\n413\tIllustrative example of two caption types for the same audio clip. Overall captions annotate speech, sound, and music, along with relative temporal order.\n414\tTimestamped captions provide precise timestamps for foreground events and speech segments.\n415\t3.1\n416\tData Sources and Filtering\n417\tCandidate clips are collected from test sets of AudioCaps, VGGSound, and MECAT, which provide complementary acoustic coverage for diverse speech, music, and environmental sound combinations while remaining comparable to widely used evaluation resources.\n418\tWe apply CED\n419\t(Dinkel\n420\tet al.\n421\t,\n422\t2024\n423\t)\n424\tto detect the presence of these modalities and retain only clips that contain cross-domain content.\n425\tFor clips containing speech, we further apply expert models to annotate speaker number and language, retaining only single-speaker English clips for reliable speech annotation.\n426\tWe additionally filter out samples with poor recording quality or audible distortion using production quality (PQ) metric\n427\t(Tjandra\n428\tet al.\n429\t,\n430\t2025\n431\t)\n432\tto ensure audio fidelity.\n433\t3.2\n434\tAnnotation Pipeline\n435\tFine-grained expert annotation.\n436\tFor speech, expert models infer speaker gender, age, accent, emotion, and transcription.\n437\tFor music, we annotate instrumentation, genre, and mood.\n438\tFor sound events, we identify foreground and background sound classes along with ambient acoustic context.\n439\tIn addition, we introduce a timestamping branch that provides precise temporal boundaries for all annotated elements across different domains.\n440\tOverall Caption.\n441\tAn LLM\n442\t(DeepSeek-AI,\n443\t2025\n444\t)\n445\taggregates annotations from multiple expert models and synthesizes them into a single coherent caption.\n446\tThe prompt instructs the model to faithfully retain expert-derived facts, reconcile contradictions, and avoid unsupported inferences.\n447\tThe LLM further leverages timestamp information to describe the temporal progression of audio elements, using phrases such as “at the beginning,” “subsequently,” or “throughout.”\n448\tThis temporal structuring is particularly important in TTA tasks, where constructing a coherent narrative of the acoustic scene is essential for generation quality.\n449\tTimestamped Caption.\n450\tIn addition to the overall description, we generate a separate timestamped caption for each clip, primarily intended for fine-grained temporal control evaluation.\n451\tThis caption shares the same core semantic content as the overall one, but differs in precise timestamps for speech segments and foreground acoustic events.\n452\tAt the event level, for sustained events such as background music, we retain only coarse temporal descriptions such as \"throughout the audio.\"\n453\tAt the clip level, we discard the timestamped caption entirely for any clip whose duration is almost entirely occupied by speech or background audio, since such samples offer limited scope for fine-grained temporal conditioning.\n454\tVoice Prompt.\n455\tFor audio samples from AudioCaps and VGGSound, we retrieve the original raw audio from YouTube.\n456\tWe first identify the longest speech segment from the audio sample to serve as the speaker reference.\n457\tSeparately, we apply voice activity detection (VAD)\n458\t(Gao\n459\tet al.\n460\t,\n461\t2023\n462\t)\n463\tto detect all speech segments in each raw audio.\n464\tThen we crop or concatenate these segments to a suitable duration, generating a set of candidate prompts.\n465\tFinally, we use a speaker verification model\n466\t(Wang\n467\tet al.\n468\t,\n469\t2023\n470\t)\n471\tto compare each candidate against the reference, retaining the top-5 most similar for manual inspection.\n472\tHuman quality control.\n473\tTo correct hallucinations introduced by model-based annotation, we perform manual inspection to ensure the quality.\n474\tThis inspection focuses on two aspects: (1) the factual completeness and accuracy of the generated captions, and (2) the selection of final voice prompts from the candidate segments.\n475\tThe manual inspection consists of three rounds: initial correction, re-check, and final spot-check.\n476\tThe final spot-check pass rate reaches approximately\n477\t90\n478\t%\n479\t90\\%\n480\t.\n481\tGiven the inherent ambiguity in certain audio categories and temporal boundaries, the pass rate provides reasonable assurance of annotation quality.\n482\t3.3\n483\tBenchmark Subsets\n484\tThe final benchmark comprises three subsets: a main set for comprehensive evaluation, and two additional subsets targeting specific capabilities.\n485\tAn illustrative example of the two caption types is shown in Figure\n486\t2\n487\t.\n488\tMain set.\n489\tThe main set contains 3,974 annotated audio clips with manually verified overall captions.\n490\tIt serves as the primary evaluation set for acoustic fidelity, speech quality, and semantic consistency.\n491\tVoice Cloning subset.\n492\tThe voice cloning subset contains 691 clips, each paired with a voice prompt and a detailed overall caption.\n493\tPrompts are 2-5 seconds, matching the prompt length range in LibriSpeech-PC for zero-shot voice cloning.\n494\tThis subset enables separate evaluation of speaker similarity in addition to the main-set metrics.\n495\tTimestamp subset.\n496\tThe timestamp subset contains approximately 1,828 clips selected from the main set, each annotated with fine-grained temporal boundaries for speech segments and foreground acoustic events.\n497\tThis subset is specifically designed to evaluate temporal control in generation.\n498\t4\n499\tEvaluation Protocol\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f533bf06-cac2-4284-a5b9-fefe61b594df", "uuid": "204f1d11-2002-40d9-a472-7371de692a73", "timestamp": "2026-08-10T06:07:53.795Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mmag_text.txt", "content": ",\n2026\n)\nproduces richer captions for mixed scenes, yet it lacks temporal ordering of sound events for generation, which would require a more comprehensive annotation pipeline.\nThe second challenge is the absence of fine-grained control mechanisms in existing benchmarks, as users in practice demand not only high-quality audio but also precise controllability over specific attributes.\nOne example is zero-shot voice cloning in TTS\n(Zhou\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, where the model mimics a speaker’s timbre from a short voice prompt.\nThis necessitates that the benchmark provide suitable prompt samples.\nHowever, selecting such segments from real-world audio is challenging due to complex acoustic scenes.\nAnother example is temporal control in TTA, which requires the model to generate sound events at precisely specified timestamps\n(Xie\net al.\n,\n2025\n)\n.\nThis requires obtaining precise timestamps for each element during the annotation process.\nTo address these challenges, we introduce MMAG, a benchmark for Multi-control Mixed Audio Generation.\nWe construct three evaluation settings that differ in conditioning signals: (1) text-only control on the main set, (2) extra speaker identity control via a short voice prompt, and (3) temporal control via timestamp-detailed captions.\nWe select cross-domain audio samples from test sets of AudioCaps, VGGSound, and MECAT.\nExtending the multi-expert annotation pipeline of MECAT with a timestamp-labeling branch, we construct detailed captions including speech transcriptions, speaker attributes (age, gender, dialect, and emotion), sound event descriptions, musical information (genre, instrument and mood) and temporal ordering.\nAfter manual inspection, the main set, voice cloning subset, and timestamp subset comprise around 3,980, 690, and 1,800 audio-text pairs respectively, with the latter two providing reference voices and precise temporal boundaries accordingly.\nTable\n1\ncompares MMAG with existing benchmarks across coverage of domains and conditions.\nUsing MMAG, we systematically evaluate representative models from these emerging system families.\nTo provide a holistic assessment of mixed audio generation, we establish an evaluation protocol across different dimensions: acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic consistency, and temporal controllability.\nOur findings reveal that existing models struggle to achieve balanced performance across different metrics.\nMoreover, introducing voice prompts leads to a noticeable performance drop, and most models exhibit little capability for fine-grained temporal control.\nOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n1.\nWe introduce\nMMAG\n, the first benchmark for\ncompositional evaluation\nof mixed audio generation. MMAG comprises a manually verified benchmark with rich annotations spanning speech, music, sound effects, and temporal relationships, together with dedicated subsets for prompt-based voice cloning and timestamp-conditioned generation.\n2.\nWe establish a unified evaluation framework that jointly measures acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic consistency and temporal control, providing a holistic assessment of mixed audio generation beyond conventional single-metric evaluation.\n3.\nWe benchmark representative agentic orchestrators, unified audio-visual models, and native mixed-audio generators under this unified protocol, revealing that current systems exhibit substantial trade-offs across generation quality and controllability, with no single model consistently excelling across all evaluation dimensions.\n2\nRelated Work\nFigure 1:\nOverview of the MMAG annotation pipeline. Expert models extract fine-grained attributes from audio clips. An LLM aggregates these annotations into coherent overall and timestamped captions, while speech segments are further processed to construct voice prompts for voice cloning evaluation. Human inspection is performed to ensure annotation quality.\nBenchmarks for audio generation\nExisting benchmarks for audio generation include AudioCaps\n(Kim\net al.\n,\n2019\n)\nand VGGSound\n(Chen\net al.\n,\n2020\n)\n, which provide large-scale real-world audio with coarse annotations.\nand MECAT\n(Niu\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, built from ACAV100M\n(Lee\net al.\n,\n2021\n)\n, which also serves as a valuable audio source and offers richer annotations through expert-model and LLM combination.\nHowever, none of these resources are designed for compositional evaluation of mixed audio generation.\nEvaluation of audio generation\nExisting metrics for audio generation have been developed along separate lines, each targeting a specific quality dimension. For distributional metrics, Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD)\n(Kilgour\net al.\n,\n2018\n)\n, Fréchet Distance (FD), Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, and Inception Score (IS)\n(Salimans\net al.\n,\n2016\n)\nare widely used to quantify how closely the generated audio matches the real distribution. Alongside these metrics, no-reference predictors such as Audiobox Aesthetics\n(Tjandra\net al.\n,\n2025\n)\nprovide perceptual quality assessments across multiple axes. For speech metrics, Word Error Rate (WER), speaker similarity (SPK-SIM), and UTMOS\n2\n2\n2\nUTMOS-v2\n(Baba\net al.\n,\n2024\n)\n.\naddress transcription accuracy, identity preservation, and naturalness respectively. For semantic metrics, Contrastive Language-Audio Pretraining (CLAP) score\n(Wu\net al.\n,\n2023\n)\nprovides global text-audio alignment, while AnyAudio-Judge\n(Li\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\nachieves fine-grained semantic evaluation by decomposing captions into atomic rubrics.\nWhile these metrics cover complementary aspects of quality, they remain scattered across isolated benchmarks, with no unified protocol for evaluating controllable mixed audio generation.\nAudio generation systems\nRecent audio generation systems can be grouped into three paradigms.\nAgentic orchestrators\n, represented in our evaluation by AuDirector\n(Ren\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, employ LLMs as central agents to invoke expert models including TangoFlux\n(Hung\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, IndexTTS2\n(Zhou\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, and MusicGen\n(Copet\net al.\n,\n2023\n)\n.\nAuDirector further incorporates critic models such as CLAP and MiMo-Audio\n(Xiaomi,\n2025\n)\nto iteratively evaluate and refine generated audio quality.\nThis training-free design enables flexible adaptation to diverse conditioning inputs, including voice references or timestamps.\nUnified audio-visual generation models\naccept diverse input modalities and synthesize synchronized audio-visual content.\nRepresentative models in our evaluation include MOVA\n(SII-OpenMOSS Team\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, Ovi\n(Low\net al.\n,\n2025\n)\n, UniAVGen\n(Zhang\net al.\n,\n2025\n)\n, LTX-2\n(HaCohen\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, and JavisDiT++\n(Liu\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, which can synthesize synchronized video alongside general audio covering speech, music, and sound effects.\nNative mixed-audio generators\n, represented by Dasheng AudioGen\n(Mei\net al.\n,\n2026\n)\n, synthesize mixed audio scenes from text.\nMing-Omni-TTS\n(Inclusion AI\net al.\n,\n2025\n)\n, a speech-centric system derived from TTS, extends its scope to joint audio-music generation.\nThese increasingly capable systems motivate a benchmark capable of evaluating multiple control dimensions simultaneously.\n3\nConstructing MMAG\nMMAG is designed to enable\ncompositional evaluation\nof mixed audio generation, where speech, music, and sound effects must be generated coherently within a single acoustic scene. Rather than introducing another collection of audio-text pairs, our construction process is guided by three principles: (1) preserving realistic mixed-audio scenarios, (2) providing rich semantic and temporal annotations for fine-grained control, and (3) enabling multi-condition control through dedicated subsets.\nFigure\n1\nillustrates the overall construction process.\nFigure 2:\nIllustrative example of two caption types for the same audio clip. Overall captions annotate speech, sound, and music, along with relative temporal order.\nTimestamped captions provide precise timestamps for foreground events and speech segments.\n3.1\nData Sources and Filtering\nCandidate clips are collected from test sets of AudioCaps, VGGSound, and MECAT, which provide complementary acoustic coverage for diverse speech, music, and environmental sound combinations while remaining comparable to widely used evaluation resources.\nWe apply CED\n(Dinkel\net al.\n,\n2024\n)\nto detect the presence of these modalities and retain only clips that contain cross-domain content.\nFor clips containing speech, we further apply expert models to annotate speaker number and language, retaining only single-speaker English clips for reliable speech annotation.\nWe additionally filter out samples with poor recording quality or audible distortion using production quality (PQ) metric\n(Tjandra\net al.\n,\n2025\n)\nto ensure audio fidelity.\n3.2\nAnnotation Pipeline\nFine-grained expert annotation.\nFor speech, expert models infer speaker gender, age, accent, emotion, and transcription.\nFor music, we annotate instrumentation, genre, and mood.\nFor sound events, we identify foreground and background sound classes along with ambient acoustic context.\nIn addition, we introduce a timestamping branch that provides precise temporal boundaries for all annotated elements across different domains.\nOverall Caption.\nAn LLM\n(DeepSeek-AI,\n2025\n)\naggregates annotations from multiple expert models and synthesizes them into a single coherent caption.\nThe prompt instructs the model to faithfully retain expert-derived facts, reconcile contradictions, and avoid unsupported inferences.\nThe LLM further leverages timestamp information to describe the temporal progression of audio elements, using phrases such as “at the beginning,” “subsequently,” or “throughout.”\nThis temporal structuring is particularly important in TTA tasks, where constructing a coherent narrative of the acoustic scene is essential for generation quality.\nTimestamped Caption.\nIn addition to the overall description, we generate a separate timestamped caption for each clip, primarily intended for fine-grained temporal control evaluation.\nThis caption shares the same core semantic content as the overall one, but differs in precise timestamps for speech segments and foreground acoustic events.\nAt the event level, for sustained events such as background music, we retain only coarse temporal descriptions such as \"throughout the audio.\"\nAt the clip level, we discard the timestamped caption entirely for any clip whose duration is almost entirely occupied by speech or background audio, since such samples offer limited scope for fine-grained temporal conditioning.\nVoice Prompt.\nFor audio samples from AudioCaps and VGGSound, we retrieve the original raw audio from YouTube.\nWe first identify the longest speech segment from the audio sample to serve as the speaker reference.\nSeparately, we apply voice activity detection (VAD)\n(Gao\net al.\n,\n2023\n)\nto detect all speech segments in each raw audio.\nThen we crop or concatenate these segments to a suitable duration, generating a set of candidate prompts.\nFinally, we use a speaker verification model\n(Wang\net al.\n,\n2023\n)\nto compare each candidate against the reference, retaining the top-5 most similar for manual inspection.\nHuman quality control.\nTo correct hallucinations introduced by model-based annotation, we perform manual inspection to ensure the quality.\nThis inspection focuses on two aspects: (1) the factual completeness and accuracy of the generated captions, and (2) the selection of final voice prompts from the candidate segments.\nThe manual inspection consists of three rounds: initial correction, re-check, and final spot-check.\nThe final spot-check pass rate reaches approximately\n90\n%\n90\\%\n.\nGiven the inherent ambiguity in certain audio categories and temporal boundaries, the pass rate provides reasonable assurance of annotation quality.\n3.3\nBenchmark Subsets\nThe final benchmark comprises three subsets: a main set for comprehensive evaluation, and two additional subsets targeting specific capabilities.\nAn illustrative example of the two caption types is shown in Figure\n2\n.\nMain set.\nThe main set contains 3,974 annotated audio clips with manually verified overall captions.\nIt serves as the primary evaluation set for acoustic fidelity, speech quality, and semantic consistency.\nVoice Cloning subset.\nThe voice cloning subset contains 691 clips, each paired with a voice prompt and a detailed overall caption.\nPrompts are 2-5 seconds, matching the prompt length range in LibriSpeech-PC for zero-shot voice cloning.\nThis subset enables separate evaluation of speaker similarity in addition to the main-set metrics.\nTimestamp subset.\nThe timestamp subset contains approximately 1,828 clips selected from the main set, each annotated with fine-grained temporal boundaries for speech segments and foreground acoustic events.\nThis subset is specifically designed to evaluate temporal control in generation.\n4\nEvaluation Protocol", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 200, "totalLines": 2810}}}
