{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-916043c195db632c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1090\tThis limitation points toward the need for more temporally aware judge models in future work.\n1091\t5.4\n1092\tError Patterns\n1093\tWe observe that partial models exhibit caption confusion: they tend to produce spoken content drawn from the caption but outside the target transcript, even with correct input formatting.\n1094\tTo quantify this phenomenon, we design a diagnostic metric\n1095\tProportion\n1096\tin Appendix\n1097\t7\n1098\t, defined as the contribution of such extraneous content to the final WER.\n1099\tA higher Proportion indicates more severe leakage, independent of the absolute WER level.\n1100\tTable 5:\n1101\tProportion (\n1102\t%\n1103\t\\%\n1104\t) on the main/timestamp set. DASG: Dasheng-AudioGen; Ming: Ming-Omni-TTS.\n1105\tModel\n1106\tPro. (Main)\n1107\tPro. (Timestamp)\n1108\tAuDirector\n1109\t1.88\n1110\t5.43\n1111\tLTX-2\n1112\t5.12\n1113\t7.41\n1114\tOvi\n1115\t13.92\n1116\t19.70\n1117\tMOVA\n1118\t4.59\n1119\t7.00\n1120\tUniAVGen\n1121\t3.75\n1122\t6.63\n1123\tDASG-Base\n1124\t18.21\n1125\t56.48\n1126\tDASG-Fine\n1127\t21.88\n1128\t36.04\n1129\tMing-0.5B\n1130\t3.78\n1131\t–\n1132\tMing-16.8B\n1133\t2.80\n1134\t–\n1135\tThe results in Table\n1136\t5\n1137\tshow that this issue is particularly pronounced in Ovi and Dasheng-AudioGen. On the main set, roughly 10\n1138\t%\n1139\t\\%\n1140\t-20\n1141\t%\n1142\t\\%\n1143\tof word errors are attributable to caption confusion. On the timestamp subset, this problem is further amplified. Dasheng-AudioGen-Base exhibits a Proportion of 56.48\n1144\t%\n1145\t\\%\n1146\t, indicating a strong correlation between increased WER and caption confusion. After adopting structured input, Dasheng-AudioGen-Fine shows substantial reductions in both WER and Proportion, though its Proportion remains higher than that of other models.\n1147\tCompared to the main set, all models show increased Proportion on the timestamp subset.\n1148\tThis suggests that the introduction of timestamps raises the difficulty of recognizing and generating accurate transcripts, highlighting the need for more diverse training data.\n1149\tDasheng-AudioGen-Fine benefits from LLM-based input processing, which significantly reduces the confusion, and AuDirector also attains a strong Proportion score via LLM-driven input processing and expert model invocation.\n1150\tLeveraging LLMs to normalize raw inputs into a form better suited for models represents an effective strategy to improve model robustness.\n1151\t6\n1152\tConclusion\n1153\tTo address the lack of comprehensive evaluation for mixed audio generation, we propose MMAG.\n1154\tIt comprises a main set of approximately 4,000 clips, a timestamp subset of roughly 1,800 clips, and a voice cloning subset of roughly 690 clips.\n1155\tMMAG is curated through a multi-stage pipeline that integrates expert models, LLM-based caption synthesis, and human quality control.\n1156\tWe have established a comprehensive evaluation framework and systematically benchmarked existing models.\n1157\tOur findings reveal that no existing model achieves balanced performance across acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic consistency, and temporal control.\n1158\tInstead, we observe systematic trade-offs between generation quality and controllability, calling for more robust and controllable generation.\n1159\tLimitations\n1160\tDespite its comprehensive scope, MMAG has several limitations.\n1161\tThe 10-second clip duration, single-speaker English restriction, and imbalanced sound/music categories limit generalizability and may bias fine-grained evaluation on underrepresented classes.\n1162\tIn addition, even with a multi-expert pipeline and manual verification, sound events, music genres, and precise timestamps remain inherently subjective, introducing unavoidable uncertainty into the ground truth.\n1163\tFor evaluation, we incorporate LLM-based semantic metrics, but current multimodal LLMs still show limited reliability on fine-grained audio tasks.\n1164\tThis is expected to improve as foundation models continue to advance.\n1165\tEthical Considerations\n1166\tWe hired external annotators for manual quality control of generated captions and voice prompt selection. All annotators were compensated at a rate above local minimum wage. Annotators received detailed instructions and were informed of their right to withdraw at any time. No personally identifiable information was collected from annotators, and the annotation task did not involve sensitive content. All audio data used in this benchmark is sourced from existing publicly available datasets (AudioCaps, VGGSound, MECAT) and is used for research purposes in accordance with their original licenses. We do not foresee direct negative societal impacts from this work; the benchmark is intended to facilitate transparent and reproducible evaluation of audio generation models.\n1167\tReferences\n1168\tK. Baba, W. Nakata, Y. Saito, and H. Saruwatari (2024)\n1169\tThe t05 system for the voicemos challenge 2024: transfer learning from deep image classifier to naturalness mos prediction of high-quality synthetic speech\n1170\t.\n1171\tIn\n1172\t2024 IEEE Spoken Language Technology Workshop (SLT)\n1173\t,\n1174\tpp. 818–824\n1175\t.\n1176\tCited by:\n1177\t§4.2\n1178\t,\n1179\tfootnote 2\n1180\t.\n1181\tM. Bain, J. Huh, T. Han, and A. Zisserman (2023)\n1182\tWhisperX: time-accurate speech transcription of long-form audio\n1183\t.\n1184\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2303.00747\n1185\t.\n1186\tCited by:\n1187\tTable 6\n1188\t,\n1189\t§4.2\n1190\t.\n1191\tF. Burkhardt, J. Wagner, H. Wierstorf, F. Eyben, and B. Schuller (2023)\n1192\tSpeech-based age and gender prediction with transformers\n1193\t.\n1194\tIn\n1195\tSpeech Communication; 15th ITG Conference\n1196\t,\n1197\tpp. 46–50\n1198\t.\n1199\tCited by:\n1200\tTable 6\n1201\t.\n1202\tH. Chen, W. Xie, A. Vedaldi, and A. Zisserman (2020)\n1203\tVGGSound: a large-scale audio-visual dataset\n1204\t.\n1205\tIn\n1206\tProceedings of ICASSP\n1207\t,\n1208\tCited by:\n1209\t§2\n1210\t.\n1211\tG. Comanici, E. Bieber, M. Schaekermann, I. Pasupat, N. Sachdeva, I. Dhillon, M. Blistein, O. Ram, D. Zhang, E. Rosen,\n1212\tet al.\n1213\t(2025)\n1214\tGemini 2.5: pushing the frontier with advanced reasoning, multimodality, long context, and next generation agentic capabilities\n1215\t.\n1216\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2507.06261\n1217\t.\n1218\tCited by:\n1219\tTable 6\n1220\t.\n1221\tJ. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, and A. Défossez (2023)\n1222\tSimple and controllable music generation\n1223\t.\n1224\tAdvances in neural information processing systems\n1225\t36\n1226\t,\n1227\tpp. 47704–47720\n1228\t.\n1229\tCited by:\n1230\t§1\n1231\t,\n1232\t§2\n1233\t.\n1234\tDeepSeek-AI (2025)\n1235\tDeepSeek-R1: incentivizing reasoning capability in LLMs via reinforcement learning\n1236\t.\n1237\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2501.12948\n1238\t.\n1239\tCited by:\n1240\t§3.2\n1241\t.\n1242\tH. Dinkel, Y. Wang, Z. Yan, J. Zhang, and Y. Wang (2024)\n1243\tCED: consistent ensemble distillation for audio tagging\n1244\t.\n1245\tIn\n1246\tICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP)\n1247\t,\n1248\tpp. 291–295\n1249\t.\n1250\tCited by:\n1251\tTable 6\n1252\t,\n1253\t§3.1\n1254\t.\n1255\tZ. Gao, Z. Li, J. Wang, H. Luo, X. Shi, M. Chen, Y. Li, L. Zuo, Z. Du, Z. Xiao,\n1256\tet al.\n1257\t(2023)\n1258\tFunasr: a fundamental end-to-end speech recognition toolkit\n1259\t.\n1260\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2305.11013\n1261\t.\n1262\tCited by:\n1263\t§3.2\n1264\t.\n1265\tJ. F. Gemmeke, D. P. W. Ellis, D. Freedman, A. Jansen, W. Lawrence, R. C. Moore, M. Plakal, and M. Ritter (2017)\n1266\tAudio Set: an ontology and human-labeled dataset for audio events\n1267\t.\n1268\tIn\n1269\tProceedings of ICASSP\n1270\t,\n1271\tCited by:\n1272\t§4.2\n1273\t.\n1274\tS. Ghosh, A. Goel, J. Kim, S. Kumar, Z. Kong, S. Lee, C. Yang, R. Duraiswami, D. Manocha, R. Valle,\n1275\tet al.\n1276\t(2026)\n1277\tAudio flamingo 3: advancing audio intelligence with fully open large audio language models\n1278\t.\n1279\tAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n1280\t38\n1281\t,\n1282\tpp. 41819–41886\n1283\t.\n1284\tCited by:\n1285\tTable 6\n1286\t.\n1287\tY. HaCohen, B. Brazowski, N. Chiprut, Y. Bitterman, A. Kvochko, and A. Berkowitz (2026)\n1288\tLTX-2: efficient joint audio-visual foundation model\n1289\t.\n1290\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2601.03233\n1291\t.\n1292\tCited by:\n1293\t§2\n1294\t.\n1295\tS. Hershey, S. Chaudhuri, D. P. W. Ellis, J. F. Gemmeke, A. Jansen, R. C. Moore, M. Plakal, D. Platt, R. A. Saurous, B. Seybold, M. Slaney, R. J. Weiss, and K. Wilson (2017)\n1296\tCNN architectures for large-scale audio classification\n1297\t.\n1298\tIn\n1299\tProceedings of ICASSP\n1300\t,\n1301\tCited by:\n1302\t§4.2\n1303\t.\n1304\tC. Hung, N. Majumder, Z. Kong, A. Mehrish, A. Zadeh, C. Li, R. Valle, B. Catanzaro, and S. Poria (2026)\n1305\tTangoflux: super fast and faithful text to audio generation with flow matching and clap-ranked preference optimization\n1306\t.\n1307\tIn\n1308\tInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n1309\t,\n1310\tVol.\n1311\t2026\n1312\t,\n1313\tpp. 150793–150816\n1314\t.\n1315\tCited by:\n1316\t§1\n1317\t,\n1318\t§2\n1319\t.\n1320\tInclusion AI, B. Gong, C. Zou, C. Zheng, C. Zhou, and C. Yan (2025)\n1321\tMing-Omni: a unified multimodal model for perception and generation\n1322\t.\n1323\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2506.09344\n1324\t.\n1325\tCited by:\n1326\t§2\n1327\t.\n1328\tJ. Kang and D. Herremans (2025)\n1329\tTowards unified music emotion recognition across dimensional and categorical models\n1330\t.\n1331\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2502.03979\n1332\t.\n1333\tCited by:\n1334\tTable 6\n1335\t.\n1336\tK. Kilgour, M. Zuluaga, D. Roblek, and M. Sharifi (2018)\n1337\tFréchet audio distance: a metric for evaluating music enhancement algorithms\n1338\t.\n1339\tarXiv preprint arXiv:1812.08466\n1340\t.\n1341\tCited by:\n1342\t§2\n1343\t.\n1344\tC. D. Kim, B. Kim, H. Lee, and G. Kim (2019)\n1345\tAudioCaps: generating captions for audios in the wild\n1346\t.\n1347\tIn\n1348\tProceedings of NAACL-HLT\n1349\t,\n1350\tCited by:\n1351\t§1\n1352\t,\n1353\t§2\n1354\t.\n1355\tS. Lee, J. Chung, Y. Yu, G. Kim, T. Breuel, G. Chechik, and Y. Song (2021)\n1356\tAcav100m: automatic curation of large-scale datasets for audio-visual video representation learning\n1357\t.\n1358\tIn\n1359\t2021 IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)\n1360\t,\n1361\tpp. 10254–10264\n1362\t.\n1363\tCited by:\n1364\t§2\n1365\t.\n1366\tH. Li, T. Tan, Y. Yang, S. Yang, and X. Chen (2026)\n1367\tAnyAudio-judge: a dynamic rubric-based benchmark and evaluator for audio instruction following\n1368\t.\n1369\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2606.03116\n1370\t.\n1371\tCited by:\n1372\t§2\n1373\t,\n1374\t§4.2\n1375\t.\n1376\tY. Li, R. Yuan, G. Zhang, Y. Ma, X. Chen, H. Yin, C. Xiao, C. Lin, A. Ragni, E. Benetos,\n1377\tet al.\n1378\t(2024)\n1379\tMert: acoustic music understanding model with large-scale self-supervised training\n1380\t.\n1381\tIn\n1382\tInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n1383\t,\n1384\tVol.\n1385\t2024\n1386\t,\n1387\tpp. 12181–12204\n1388\t.\n1389\tCited by:\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f533bf06-cac2-4284-a5b9-fefe61b594df", "uuid": "270d613f-aa19-4a2f-8673-25d58c849b34", "timestamp": "2026-08-10T06:09:28.567Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mmag_text.txt", "content": "This limitation points toward the need for more temporally aware judge models in future work.\n5.4\nError Patterns\nWe observe that partial models exhibit caption confusion: they tend to produce spoken content drawn from the caption but outside the target transcript, even with correct input formatting.\nTo quantify this phenomenon, we design a diagnostic metric\nProportion\nin Appendix\n7\n, defined as the contribution of such extraneous content to the final WER.\nA higher Proportion indicates more severe leakage, independent of the absolute WER level.\nTable 5:\nProportion (\n%\n\\%\n) on the main/timestamp set. DASG: Dasheng-AudioGen; Ming: Ming-Omni-TTS.\nModel\nPro. (Main)\nPro. (Timestamp)\nAuDirector\n1.88\n5.43\nLTX-2\n5.12\n7.41\nOvi\n13.92\n19.70\nMOVA\n4.59\n7.00\nUniAVGen\n3.75\n6.63\nDASG-Base\n18.21\n56.48\nDASG-Fine\n21.88\n36.04\nMing-0.5B\n3.78\n–\nMing-16.8B\n2.80\n–\nThe results in Table\n5\nshow that this issue is particularly pronounced in Ovi and Dasheng-AudioGen. On the main set, roughly 10\n%\n\\%\n-20\n%\n\\%\nof word errors are attributable to caption confusion. On the timestamp subset, this problem is further amplified. Dasheng-AudioGen-Base exhibits a Proportion of 56.48\n%\n\\%\n, indicating a strong correlation between increased WER and caption confusion. After adopting structured input, Dasheng-AudioGen-Fine shows substantial reductions in both WER and Proportion, though its Proportion remains higher than that of other models.\nCompared to the main set, all models show increased Proportion on the timestamp subset.\nThis suggests that the introduction of timestamps raises the difficulty of recognizing and generating accurate transcripts, highlighting the need for more diverse training data.\nDasheng-AudioGen-Fine benefits from LLM-based input processing, which significantly reduces the confusion, and AuDirector also attains a strong Proportion score via LLM-driven input processing and expert model invocation.\nLeveraging LLMs to normalize raw inputs into a form better suited for models represents an effective strategy to improve model robustness.\n6\nConclusion\nTo address the lack of comprehensive evaluation for mixed audio generation, we propose MMAG.\nIt comprises a main set of approximately 4,000 clips, a timestamp subset of roughly 1,800 clips, and a voice cloning subset of roughly 690 clips.\nMMAG is curated through a multi-stage pipeline that integrates expert models, LLM-based caption synthesis, and human quality control.\nWe have established a comprehensive evaluation framework and systematically benchmarked existing models.\nOur findings reveal that no existing model achieves balanced performance across acoustic fidelity, speech quality, semantic consistency, and temporal control.\nInstead, we observe systematic trade-offs between generation quality and controllability, calling for more robust and controllable generation.\nLimitations\nDespite its comprehensive scope, MMAG has several limitations.\nThe 10-second clip duration, single-speaker English restriction, and imbalanced sound/music categories limit generalizability and may bias fine-grained evaluation on underrepresented classes.\nIn addition, even with a multi-expert pipeline and manual verification, sound events, music genres, and precise timestamps remain inherently subjective, introducing unavoidable uncertainty into the ground truth.\nFor evaluation, we incorporate LLM-based semantic metrics, but current multimodal LLMs still show limited reliability on fine-grained audio tasks.\nThis is expected to improve as foundation models continue to advance.\nEthical Considerations\nWe hired external annotators for manual quality control of generated captions and voice prompt selection. All annotators were compensated at a rate above local minimum wage. Annotators received detailed instructions and were informed of their right to withdraw at any time. No personally identifiable information was collected from annotators, and the annotation task did not involve sensitive content. All audio data used in this benchmark is sourced from existing publicly available datasets (AudioCaps, VGGSound, MECAT) and is used for research purposes in accordance with their original licenses. We do not foresee direct negative societal impacts from this work; the benchmark is intended to facilitate transparent and reproducible evaluation of audio generation models.\nReferences\nK. Baba, W. Nakata, Y. Saito, and H. Saruwatari (2024)\nThe t05 system for the voicemos challenge 2024: transfer learning from deep image classifier to naturalness mos prediction of high-quality synthetic speech\n.\nIn\n2024 IEEE Spoken Language Technology Workshop (SLT)\n,\npp. 818–824\n.\nCited by:\n§4.2\n,\nfootnote 2\n.\nM. Bain, J. Huh, T. Han, and A. Zisserman (2023)\nWhisperX: time-accurate speech transcription of long-form audio\n.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2303.00747\n.\nCited by:\nTable 6\n,\n§4.2\n.\nF. Burkhardt, J. Wagner, H. Wierstorf, F. Eyben, and B. Schuller (2023)\nSpeech-based age and gender prediction with transformers\n.\nIn\nSpeech Communication; 15th ITG Conference\n,\npp. 46–50\n.\nCited by:\nTable 6\n.\nH. Chen, W. Xie, A. Vedaldi, and A. Zisserman (2020)\nVGGSound: a large-scale audio-visual dataset\n.\nIn\nProceedings of ICASSP\n,\nCited by:\n§2\n.\nG. Comanici, E. Bieber, M. Schaekermann, I. Pasupat, N. Sachdeva, I. Dhillon, M. Blistein, O. Ram, D. Zhang, E. Rosen,\net al.\n(2025)\nGemini 2.5: pushing the frontier with advanced reasoning, multimodality, long context, and next generation agentic capabilities\n.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2507.06261\n.\nCited by:\nTable 6\n.\nJ. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, and A. Défossez (2023)\nSimple and controllable music generation\n.\nAdvances in neural information processing systems\n36\n,\npp. 47704–47720\n.\nCited by:\n§1\n,\n§2\n.\nDeepSeek-AI (2025)\nDeepSeek-R1: incentivizing reasoning capability in LLMs via reinforcement learning\n.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2501.12948\n.\nCited by:\n§3.2\n.\nH. Dinkel, Y. Wang, Z. Yan, J. Zhang, and Y. Wang (2024)\nCED: consistent ensemble distillation for audio tagging\n.\nIn\nICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP)\n,\npp. 291–295\n.\nCited by:\nTable 6\n,\n§3.1\n.\nZ. Gao, Z. Li, J. Wang, H. Luo, X. Shi, M. Chen, Y. Li, L. Zuo, Z. Du, Z. Xiao,\net al.\n(2023)\nFunasr: a fundamental end-to-end speech recognition toolkit\n.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2305.11013\n.\nCited by:\n§3.2\n.\nJ. F. Gemmeke, D. P. W. Ellis, D. Freedman, A. Jansen, W. Lawrence, R. C. Moore, M. Plakal, and M. Ritter (2017)\nAudio Set: an ontology and human-labeled dataset for audio events\n.\nIn\nProceedings of ICASSP\n,\nCited by:\n§4.2\n.\nS. Ghosh, A. Goel, J. Kim, S. Kumar, Z. Kong, S. Lee, C. Yang, R. Duraiswami, D. Manocha, R. Valle,\net al.\n(2026)\nAudio flamingo 3: advancing audio intelligence with fully open large audio language models\n.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n38\n,\npp. 41819–41886\n.\nCited by:\nTable 6\n.\nY. HaCohen, B. Brazowski, N. Chiprut, Y. Bitterman, A. Kvochko, and A. Berkowitz (2026)\nLTX-2: efficient joint audio-visual foundation model\n.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2601.03233\n.\nCited by:\n§2\n.\nS. Hershey, S. Chaudhuri, D. P. W. Ellis, J. F. Gemmeke, A. Jansen, R. C. Moore, M. Plakal, D. Platt, R. A. Saurous, B. Seybold, M. Slaney, R. J. Weiss, and K. Wilson (2017)\nCNN architectures for large-scale audio classification\n.\nIn\nProceedings of ICASSP\n,\nCited by:\n§4.2\n.\nC. Hung, N. Majumder, Z. Kong, A. Mehrish, A. Zadeh, C. Li, R. Valle, B. Catanzaro, and S. Poria (2026)\nTangoflux: super fast and faithful text to audio generation with flow matching and clap-ranked preference optimization\n.\nIn\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n,\nVol.\n2026\n,\npp. 150793–150816\n.\nCited by:\n§1\n,\n§2\n.\nInclusion AI, B. Gong, C. Zou, C. Zheng, C. Zhou, and C. Yan (2025)\nMing-Omni: