{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8cb0750ca4f6fff5", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2280\t.\n2281\tThe\n2282\tspeech\n2283\tcontent\n2284\tmay\n2285\tbe\n2286\tsummarized\n2287\tat\n2288\tthe\n2289\tbeginning\n2290\t,\n2291\tbut\n2292\tthe\n2293\tcomplete\n2294\ttranscription\n2295\tmust\n2296\tappear\n2297\tsubsequently\n2298\t.\n2299\t-\n2300\tExample\n2301\t:\n2302\t\"\n2303\tA\n2304\t20-30\n2305\tyoung\n2306\tmale\n2307\tspeaker\n2308\tdelivers\n2309\tan\n2310\temotionally\n2311\tcharged\n2312\tEnglish\n2313\tmonologue\n2314\texpressing\n2315\tpride\n2316\t,\n2317\t’\n2318\tI\n2319\tlove\n2320\tmy\n2321\tcountry\n2322\tdeeply\n2323\t’.\n2324\tAccompanied\n2325\tby\n2326\tdark\n2327\t-\n2328\ttoned\n2329\telectronic\n2330\tmusic\n2331\twith\n2332\tsteady\n2333\tpercussion\n2334\t.\n2335\tVehicle\n2336\tengine\n2337\tnoise\n2338\tpersists\n2339\tthroughout\n2340\tthe\n2341\trecording\n2342\t.\"\n2343\t2)\n2344\tTimestamped\n2345\tCaption\n2346\t(\n2347\tseveral\n2348\tsentences\n2349\t):\n2350\t-\n2351\tInclude\n2352\tas\n2353\tmuch\n2354\tinformation\n2355\tas\n2356\tpossible\n2357\t,\n2358\tcovering\n2359\tspeech\n2360\t,\n2361\tmusic\n2362\t,\n2363\tsound\n2364\t,\n2365\tenvironment\n2366\t,\n2367\tand\n2368\tother\n2369\trelevant\n2370\tdimensions\n2371\t.\n2372\t-\n2373\tIf\n2374\ttranscription\n2375\texists\n2376\t,\n2377\tinclude\n2378\tthe\n2379\tcomplete\n2380\ttranscription\n2381\tverbatim\n2382\t,\n2383\tenclosed\n2384\tin\n2385\tquotation\n2386\tmarks\n2387\t.\n2388\t-\n2389\tProvide\n2390\tspecific\n2391\ttimestamps\n2392\tfor\n2393\teach\n2394\tsentence\n2395\tand\n2396\tforeground\n2397\tevent\n2398\tby\n2399\tintegrating\n2400\ttranscription\n2401\t-\n2402\tsegments\n2403\tand\n2404\tevent_segments\n2405\tdata\n2406\t.\n2407\tIdentify\n2408\tall\n2409\tforeground\n2410\tevents\n2411\t,\n2412\tmerging\n2413\tthose\n2414\twith\n2415\tsimilar\n2416\tcategories\n2417\tor\n2418\thighly\n2419\toverlapping\n2420\ttime\n2421\tintervals\n2422\t.\n2423\t-\n2424\tFor\n2425\tbackground\n2426\tsound\n2427\tevents\n2428\t,\n2429\tdo\n2430\tnot\n2431\tprovide\n2432\tprecise\n2433\ttimestamps\n2434\t;\n2435\tinstead\n2436\t,\n2437\tdescribe\n2438\ttheir\n2439\toccurrence\n2440\tusing\n2441\tcoarse\n2442\t-\n2443\tgrained\n2444\tterms\n2445\tsuch\n2446\tas\n2447\t\"\n2448\tstart\n2449\t\",\n2450\t\"\n2451\tend\n2452\t\",\n2453\t\"\n2454\tmiddle\n2455\t\",\n2456\tor\n2457\t\"\n2458\tentire\n2459\tprocess\n2460\t\".\n2461\t-\n2462\tExample\n2463\t:\n2464\t\"\n2465\tIn\n2466\t0.2-1.5\n2467\ts\n2468\t,\n2469\ta\n2470\t20-30\n2471\tyoung\n2472\tfemale\n2473\tspeaker\n2474\tdelivers\n2475\tan\n2476\temotionally\n2477\tcharged\n2478\tEnglish\n2479\tmonologue\n2480\texpressing\n2481\tpride\n2482\t,\n2483\t’\n2484\tI\n2485\tlove\n2486\tmy\n2487\tcountry\n2488\tdeeply\n2489\t’.\n2490\tThen\n2491\tapplause\n2492\terupted\n2493\tbetween\n2494\t2.1-5.9\n2495\tseconds\n2496\t.\n2497\tIn\n2498\t7.5-9.4\n2499\ts\n2500\t,\n2501\tthe\n2502\tspeaker\n2503\tsaid\n2504\t,\n2505\t’\n2506\tmy\n2507\tspeech\n2508\tis\n2509\tover\n2510\t,\n2511\tthank\n2512\tyou\n2513\tall\n2514\t.’\n2515\tThere\n2516\tis\n2517\tdark\n2518\telectronic\n2519\tmusic\n2520\tand\n2521\tstable\n2522\tpercussion\n2523\tin\n2524\tthe\n2525\tbackground\n2526\t.\"\n2527\tAppendix C\n2528\tDataset Details\n2529\tWe perform a comprehensive statistical analysis of the benchmark to characterize its composition and coverage. Figure\n2530\t4\n2531\tpresents the label distributions on the main set across multiple dimensions, including gender, age group, emotion type, and accent category.\n2532\tFor the timestamp subset, we report the proportion of samples with precise temporal annotations, covering both speech segments and foreground acoustic events.\n2533\tTo examine musical diversity, we extract instrument information from the generated captions using an LLM. Figure\n2534\t3\n2535\tpresents the per-clip instrument count distribution, revealing the varying instrumental complexity across samples.\n2536\tFigure 3:\n2537\tInstrument distribution of MMAG\n2538\tFor sound events, we extract event types from the captions via the same LLM, yielding roughly 320 distinct categories after excluding speech- and music-related events.\n2539\tIn comparison, after removing ’speech,’ ’music,’ and their descendants from the AudioSet ontology, roughly 360 categories remain.\n2540\tThe substantial overlap between MMAG and AudioSet demonstrates that our benchmark achieves broad coverage of diverse sound events.\n2541\tFigure 4:\n2542\tLabel distribution of MMAG\n2543\tFigure 5:\n2544\tSound event distribution of MMAG\n2545\tFigure 6:\n2546\tFrequency distribution of sound events\n2547\tHowever, as shown in the Figure\n2548\t5\n2549\tand Figure\n2550\t6\n2551\t, the distribution of sound events in MMAG is not perfectly balanced.\n2552\tThis skew largely reflects the natural prevalence of certain event types in real-world recordings, inherited from the underlying data sources.\n2553\tWhile this imbalance presents challenges for evaluating performance on rare categories, it also more faithfully mirrors the long-tail distribution characteristic of real acoustic environments.\n2554\tFor the voice cloning subset, we compute the SPK-SIM between each voice prompt and its corresponding target audio sample, achieving an average score of approximately 0.82. By comparison, the reference score computed on the LibriSpeech-pc dataset is approximately 0.86.\n2555\tThe slightly lower performance on our subset can be attributed to the presence of background acoustic content in some of the extracted voice prompts, which inevitably interferes with speaker embedding extraction and reduces the similarity score.\n2556\tHowever, such background-accompanied prompts are inherently reasonable in the context of mixed audio generation, where the model is expected to extract and preserve the target speaker’s timbre not only from perfectly clean speech, but also from realistic, non-isolated audio segments.\n2557\tFrom the SPK-SIM evaluation perspective, WeSpeaker remains generally robust to background speech and environmental noise. While not perfect in complex acoustic scenes, the metric still provides a meaningful measure of speaker similarity.\n2558\tAppendix D\n2559\tAdditional Results\n2560\tTable 7:\n2561\tComparison across three benchmark subsets. Best results within each subset are highlighted in\n2562\tbold\n2563\tand underlined.\n2564\tCategory\n2565\tModel\n2566\tAux. Metric\n2567\tWER Any.\n2568\tFD\n2569\tCNN14\n2570\t{}_{\\text{CNN14}}\n2571\t↓\n2572\t\\downarrow\n2573\tCE\n2574\t↑\n2575\t\\uparrow\n2576\tCU\n2577\t↑\n2578\t\\uparrow\n2579\tHit Rate (%)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f533bf06-cac2-4284-a5b9-fefe61b594df", "uuid": "9f3ee258-d9d1-44f2-83f1-a7647b47166c", "timestamp": "2026-08-10T06:11:04.555Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mmag_text.txt", "content": ".\nThe\nspeech\ncontent\nmay\nbe\nsummarized\nat\nthe\nbeginning\n,\nbut\nthe\ncomplete\ntranscription\nmust\nappear\nsubsequently\n.\n-\nExample\n:\n\"\nA\n20-30\nyoung\nmale\nspeaker\ndelivers\nan\nemotionally\ncharged\nEnglish\nmonologue\nexpressing\npride\n,\n’\nI\nlove\nmy\ncountry\ndeeply\n’.\nAccompanied\nby\ndark\n-\ntoned\nelectronic\nmusic\nwith\nsteady\npercussion\n.\nVehicle\nengine\nnoise\npersists\nthroughout\nthe\nrecording\n.\"\n2)\nTimestamped\nCaption\n(\nseveral\nsentences\n):\n-\nInclude\nas\nmuch\ninformation\nas\npossible\n,\ncovering\nspeech\n,\nmusic\n,\nsound\n,\nenvironment\n,\nand\nother\nrelevant\ndimensions\n.\n-\nIf\ntranscription\nexists\n,\ninclude\nthe\ncomplete\ntranscription\nverbatim\n,\nenclosed\nin\nquotation\nmarks\n.\n-\nProvide\nspecific\ntimestamps\nfor\neach\nsentence\nand\nforeground\nevent\nby\nintegrating\ntranscription\n-\nsegments\nand\nevent_segments\ndata\n.\nIdentify\nall\nforeground\nevents\n,\nmerging\nthose\nwith\nsimilar\ncategories\nor\nhighly\noverlapping\ntime\nintervals\n.\n-\nFor\nbackground\nsound\nevents\n,\ndo\nnot\nprovide\nprecise\ntimestamps\n;\ninstead\n,\ndescribe\ntheir\noccurrence\nusing\ncoarse\n-\ngrained\nterms\nsuch\nas\n\"\nstart\n\",\n\"\nend\n\",\n\"\nmiddle\n\",\nor\n\"\nentire\nprocess\n\".\n-\nExample\n:\n\"\nIn\n0.2-1.5\ns\n,\na\n20-30\nyoung\nfemale\nspeaker\ndelivers\nan\nemotionally\ncharged\nEnglish\nmonologue\nexpressing\npride\n,\n’\nI\nlove\nmy\ncountry\ndeeply\n’.\nThen\napplause\nerupted\nbetween\n2.1-5.9\nseconds\n.\nIn\n7.5-9.4\ns\n,\nthe\nspeaker\nsaid\n,\n’\nmy\nspeech\nis\nover\n,\nthank\nyou\nall\n.’\nThere\nis\ndark\nelectronic\nmusic\nand\nstable\npercussion\nin\nthe\nbackground\n.\"\nAppendix C\nDataset Details\nWe perform a comprehensive statistical analysis of the benchmark to characterize its composition and coverage. Figure\n4\npresents the label distributions on the main set across multiple dimensions, including gender, age group, emotion type, and accent category.\nFor the timestamp subset, we report the proportion of samples with precise temporal annotations, covering both speech segments and foreground acoustic events.\nTo examine musical diversity, we extract instrument information from the generated captions using an LLM. Figure\n3\npresents the per-clip instrument count distribution, revealing the varying instrumental complexity across samples.\nFigure 3:\nInstrument distribution of MMAG\nFor sound events, we extract event types from the captions via the same LLM, yielding roughly 320 distinct categories after excluding speech- and music-related events.\nIn comparison, after removing ’speech,’ ’music,’ and their descendants from the AudioSet ontology, roughly 360 categories remain.\nThe substantial overlap between MMAG and AudioSet demonstrates that our benchmark achieves broad coverage of diverse sound events.\nFigure 4:\nLabel distribution of MMAG\nFigure 5:\nSound event distribution of MMAG\nFigure 6:\nFrequency distribution of sound events\nHowever, as shown in the Figure\n5\nand Figure\n6\n, the distribution of sound events in MMAG is not perfectly balanced.\nThis skew largely reflects the natural prevalence of certain event types in real-world recordings, inherited from the underlying data sources.\nWhile this imbalance presents challenges for evaluating performance on rare categories, it also more faithfully mirrors the long-tail distribution characteristic of real acoustic environments.\nFor the voice cloning subset, we compute the SPK-SIM between each voice prompt and its corresponding target audio sample, achieving an average score of approximately 0.82. By comparison, the reference score computed on the LibriSpeech-pc dataset is approximately 0.86.\nThe slightly lower performance on our subset can be attributed to the presence of background acoustic content in some of the extracted voice prompts, which inevitably interferes with speaker embedding extraction and reduces the similarity score.\nHowever, such background-accompanied prompts are inherently reasonable in the context of mixed audio generation, where the model is expected to extract and preserve the target speaker’s timbre not only from perfectly clean speech, but also from realistic, non-isolated audio segments.\nFrom the SPK-SIM evaluation perspective, WeSpeaker remains generally robust to background speech and environmental noise. While not perfect in complex acoustic scenes, the metric still provides a meaningful measure of speaker similarity.\nAppendix D\nAdditional Results\nTable 7:\nComparison across three benchmark subsets. Best results within each subset are highlighted in\nbold\nand underlined.\nCategory\nModel\nAux. Metric\nWER Any.\nFD\nCNN14\n{}_{\\text{CNN14}}\n↓\n\\downarrow\nCE\n↑\n\\uparrow\nCU\n↑\n\\uparrow\nHit Rate (%)", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 2280, "totalLines": 2810}}}
